## Intuition
The problem asks us to rearrange a singly linked list so that all nodes at odd positions come first, followed by nodes at even positions. The key insight is that we don't need extra space — we can simply rewire the next pointers to separate odd and even nodes, then connect the odd list to the even list at the end.

## Approach
1. Handle edge cases: if the list is empty or has only one node, 2. return it directly.
- Maintain two pointers:
- odd_head to track the current odd node.
- even_head to track the current even node.
- Keep a reference to the first even node (first_even) so we can 3. attach it later.
- Traverse the list:
- Link the current odd node to the next odd node.
- Link the current even node to the next even node.
- Move both pointers forward.
4. After traversal, connect the last odd node to the head of the even list (first_even).
This ensures all odd-positioned nodes are grouped together, followed by all even-positioned nodes.


